WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.900
Film Courage: How has the screenwriting Master’s
program transformed your screenwriting process?

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:06.130
What do you do differently now?

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:10.470
Marty Lang, Screenwriter/Professor/Director:
Well I think a lot more about the structure

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:16.070
from the very beginning then the way that
I used to do it.

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:22.689
Previously if I had an idea for a movie I
would just start writing and just sort of

00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:26.689
see where it went and sometimes good things
would come from that.

00:00:26.689 --> 00:00:31.019
There definitely is a benefit to sort of free
writing and just kind of seeing what comes

00:00:31.019 --> 00:00:32.450
from it.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:40.000
But my experience has taught me that spending
time to outline an idea before actually writing

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.130
it makes the writing much more efficient.

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:49.540
I’ve saved multiple drafts of screenplays
because I’ve gone and outlined ahead of

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:55.579
time and I figured out the major turning points
of the film and that sort of thing.

00:00:55.579 --> 00:01:00.650
That’s something I really didn’t do beforehand
because I sort of rebelled against it.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:05.469
I thought that structure is the enemy and
the thing that I’ve learned is that structure

00:01:05.469 --> 00:01:07.240
is your friend.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:14.479
It helps point our errors before you get to
them and it helps you stay on track.

00:01:14.479 --> 00:01:19.399
If you know what the next thing is that you’re
writing towards, it’s a lot better if it’s

00:01:19.399 --> 00:01:23.539
five pages away then if it’s 50 pages.

00:01:23.539 --> 00:01:30.039
So you know you have a specific thing to write
to, your scenes have more urgency, your scenes

00:01:30.039 --> 00:01:34.240
have more conflict as a result of that.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.179
So I think that’s the biggest thing that
I’ve learned.

00:01:37.179 --> 00:01:41.729
The other thing that I sort of picked up along
the way is that the more personal you can

00:01:41.729 --> 00:01:46.759
make your stories and the more raw you can
make them I think the more effective they

00:01:46.759 --> 00:01:49.079
are for the reader.

00:01:49.079 --> 00:01:54.060
The story that I wrote about my dad, there
were some really dark things that were in

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.060
there.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:56.409
And it’s not a dark film per se.

00:01:56.409 --> 00:02:05.729
It’s a drama but really laying there some
emotional things that I had dealt with a long

00:02:05.729 --> 00:02:09.540
time ago, it really helps with the writing.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:15.720
It makes it more engaging, it makes it more
interesting for the reader.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:22.670
The more you try to cover up sort of the raw
things that your characters are dealing with

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:24.850
the less interesting it becomes.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.440
So I think it’s kind of good to lay it all
out there.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:28.970
It’s kind of a form of free therapy.

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:33.970
Film Courage: I know you’d said in another
class from another professor that they talked

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:39.780
about deciding what kind of protagonist you
really want to focus on.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:43.210
Did you start to see parallels in who you
wanted to write about?

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:49.930
Marty: And you know it’s funny, I have and
I see it now in the film that I’m making

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:55.370
now that it really is...even though the characters
in this film that we’re making now have

00:02:55.370 --> 00:03:01.151
nothing to do with me and my time in my life
because they’re all in their 20’s, their

00:03:01.151 --> 00:03:04.020
struggles are exactly what I’m dealing with
now.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:10.970
It really is interesting how subliminally
you sort of inform your work with the stuff

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:15.951
that you are dealing with in your life and
the issues that you really care about in end

00:03:15.951 --> 00:03:17.740
that you struggle with.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:19.230
It’s really an interesting thing.

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:22.330
I thought I was trying to get away from that
but you really can’t.

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:23.580
It really was there.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:27.840
Film Courage: So you see that a lot of the
movies that you’ve been drawn toward have

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.280
a similar protagonist?

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:30.730
Marty: I think so.

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:38.540
You know, please that are trying to do good
but have come from really hardscrabble, really

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:44.510
struggling circumstances, you know either
emotionally or socioeconomically, those are

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:48.270
the kinds of characters that I really gravitate
to.

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:54.740
One of my favorite films ever was a film called
SHORT-TERM 12 that was Brie Larson’s first

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:58.990
real leading role before she got into A ROOM
that won her the Oscar.

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:05.310
And that film is about a woman who works at
a home for troubled teens and she herself

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:11.370
was a troubled teen when she was growing up
so she sort fo enmeshed in that world and

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:13.500
she’s struggling to get out of it.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:19.100
But it’s hard because you are surrounded
by all this trauma.

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:24.240
You know, that really resonated with me because
sometimes it’s hard to break out of that

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:30.090
kind of life when you grow up on it and eventually
you know here character she does move on in

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:32.030
way.

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:34.520
So that was a film I really loved.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:40.950
So those kind of character you know that are
really struggling with moving on from a world

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:45.900
that they know even though it’s negative,
that kind of story really connects with me.

